Materials and methods Nanoparticle characterization FSNPs were synthesized according to the method outlined by Songvorawit, et al. 15  using the water-in-oil reverse microemulsion technique, and Rubpy dye (tris [2,2′-bipyridyl]ruthenium[II] chloride) as a fluorophore. The NPs were examined under a JEM-1400 transmission electron microscope (TEM; JEOL, Tokyo, Japan), and their size was measured from the TEM images employing the ImageJ software version 1.53o (NIH, USA;  https://imagej.nih.gov/ij ). Since this study used FSNPs suspended in saline water, their dispersion stability was examined in artificial seawater (ASTM D1141-98, pH 8.2) utilizing a Nano ZSP zeta-sizer (Malvern Panalytical, Great Malvern, England). Emission spectra were examined employing a Duetta ™  fluorometer (Horiba Scientific, Kyoto, Japan) at excitation bands of 365 and 460 nm, which corresponded to the UV light wavelength used to track animal behavior in this study and the maximal excitation wavelength of Rubpy dye, respectively 14 . The maximal emission wavelength of the FSNPs was calculated using a moving average based on five data points. Test animals and handling Commercial brine shrimp ( Artemia franciscana ) cysts (American Eagle, USA) imported from the Great Salt Lake, USA were purchased from a local supplier in Bangkok, Thailand. They were hatched in artificial seawater at 25–26 °C under continuous aeration and illumination. For rearing adult shrimp, nauplii (shrimp larvae) were transferred into an 8 L tank and raised in filtered natural seawater (3% salinity) at 25–26 °C under continuous aeration and a light intensity of 1,600 lx (fluorescent lamp) under a 12/12 h illumination cycle. Artificial food (composition unknown, branded as N.P. Guppy, Thailand) purchased from a local seller in Bangkok, Thailand, was provided every other day, and the adults were obtained 3 weeks after hatching. Euthanasia was performed utilizing the two-step method (immersion in 5% ethanol for 5 min and then 10% formalin) per the AVMA guidelines 41 . The experimental protocol was approved by the Animal Care and Use Committee of the Faculty of Science, Chulalongkorn University (Protocol Review No. 2423018). Acute toxicity test Nauplii, 18–24 h post-hatching, were transferred into 24-well plates containing 1–1,024 mg/L of FSNP suspension in artificial seawater (2 mL of suspension and 10–20 nauplii per well) and cultured at 25–26 °C under a light intensity of 1,600 lx (fluorescent lamp) with a 12/12 h light/dark cycle. Brine shrimp grown in artificial seawater without FSNPs were used as a control. Mortality and immobility were checked at 24 and 48 h after exposure. A nauplius was considered dead if its appendages stopped moving within 10 s. Immobility was identified when a nauplius remained with moving appendages in situ for 10 s and was calculated by dividing the number of immobile (but still alive) individuals by the total number of living individuals. A mortality rate in the 24-h period of less than 10% was considered valid for the control group. Each treatment was conducted in eight replications. Test for the appropriate FSNP concentration for tracking To ensure that all tested brine shrimp were ready to eat the food provided, 15–30 nauplii, 24–36 h after hatching were used. They were incubated in 2 mL of FSNP suspension in 24-well plates for 1 h at 25–26 °C in the dark under two conditions: food presence (1 × 10 6  cells/mL of baker’s yeast) or food absence. They were then euthanized. The fluorescence due to FSNP intake, which was visible through the body wall, was inspected under a stereo microscope coupled with a UV lamp set to 365 nm. To minimize observer bias, the same examiner conducted all observations throughout the experiment. The fluorescence brightness was scored on a scale ranging from 0 to 2: 0 = no, 1 = slight, and 2 = bright fluorescence (Fig.  2 ). The marking efficiency was calculated following the formula described previously 15 , 42 , as shown in Eq. ( 