				\begin{document}$$\text{Feeding}\,\text{rate}={100}\times\frac{{C}{0}-{C}{t}}{({t}\times{N})}$$\end{document} where  C 0  and  C t  are the density of yeast (cells/mL) at the beginning and end of the experiment, respectively,  t  is the incubation period (h), and  N  is the number of brine shrimp in the vial. Each treatment was conducted in seven replications. Persistence of FSNPs in brine shrimp A depuration experiment was conducted under food presence or absence. Baker’s yeast powder was used as food. Nauplii, 18–24 h after hatching, were dark-incubated in a suspension mixture of 16 mg/L FSNPs and 1 × 10 6  cells/mL of baker’s yeast for 1 h. Then, the nauplii were washed with seawater and passed through a 110 μm net. Next, 5–10 nauplii were transferred into each well of a 24-well plate containing 2 mL of seawater and 1 × 10 6  cells/mL baker’s yeast. After euthanasia, the fluorescence of FSNPs consumed by the nauplii was observed under a stereo microscope equipped with a UV lamp and an Axioscope 5 fluorescence microscope (ZEISS, Oberkochen, Germany) at 0, 15, 30, 45, and 60 min, as well as 2, 4, 8, 12, and 24 h. The number of nauplii with observable FSNPs was counted irrespective of the fluorescence brightness. Under food absence, the entire experiment was conducted with the same procedure, but without yeast. Each treatment was conducted in eight replications. To examine whether FSNPs moved along with the ingested food, another experiment similar to the depuration described above was conducted by providing brine shrimp nauplii with DAPI-stained baker’s yeast as food and FSNPs simultaneously. The nauplii were then examined under a fluorescence microscope. Chronic toxicity test Based on the results of the appropriate FSNP concentration experiment, a concentration of 16 mg/L FSNPs was used for the chronic toxicity test. Brine shrimp nauplii (18–24 h post-hatching) were transferred to 250 mL Erlenmeyer flasks containing 150 mL of artificial seawater under continuous aeration, with 10 nauplii per flask. They were fed with artificial food, and both seawater and food were renewed every 48 h. Three treatment groups were performed: (1) continuous exposure to FSNPs, (2) 3-hour exposure to FSNPs every 48 h, and (3) 6-hour exposure to FSNPs every 48 h. In treatments (2) and (3), nauplii were exposed to FSNPs prior to the renewal of seawater and food. The experiment was conducted over a period of 14 days. A control group was maintained in artificial seawater without FSNPs. Rearing conditions and mortality assessment criteria were consistent with those used in the acute toxicity test. Each treatment was conducted in nine replications. Test with adult brine shrimp Adult brine shrimp, 25 males and 24 females, were used to evaluate whether FSNPs could be applied to developed stages. The sexes were determined by the presence of a clasping organ (male) and brood sac (female). The shrimp were randomly selected from an aquarium and transferred into 24-well plates filled with 2 mL of baker’s yeast suspension (1 × 10 6  cells/mL), one individual per well. They were dark-acclimatized for 30 min. The FSNP suspension was then added to each well to reach a final NP concentration of 16 mg/L. Observations were made every 5 min under UV light to detect the appearance of abdominal fluorescence, followed by further monitoring until luminescent feces were detected, indicating complete gut passage of the ingested FSNPs. Statistical analyses Because our data did not meet the assumption for parametric statistics, nonparametric statistical analyses were used in all tests. The effects of FSNP concentration on mortality and immobility were ascertained by applying the Kruskal–Wallis test; Dunn’s test was used for pairwise comparison. The effects of FSNP concentration, the presence or absence of food, and their interaction with the marking efficiency or fluorescence brightness of the marked individuals were analyzed employing the aligned rank test. Feeding rates in the presence and absence of FSNPs were corrected for the rate of yeast autolysis in the control group and then compared by the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. The effects of depuration time, the presence or absence of food, and their interactions were analyzed using the aligned rank and Dunn’s tests. Locomotion data for shrimp treated with FSNPs were compared with the control utilizing the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. The number of times that the abdomen of adult brine shrimp exhibited luminescence and the number of times that the fluorescent feces appeared due to the excretion of FSNPs were compared between sexes using the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. LT 50  was analyzed using probit analysis in SPSS version 28.0 44 . All other statistical analyses were performed using the base, ARTool, combinate, dplyr, survival, survminer, and tidyverse packages in R, version 4.3.0 45 .

